WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.760
Slack was the king.
Explosive growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.880
over a billion in funding, a billion&nbsp;
dollar valuation in 8 months.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.960
But most of all, users loved it.
Slack practically invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.920
messaging for businesses.
But now, it’s being replaced.&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:23.280
Things were going too well, so&nbsp;
along came Microsoft to the rescue.&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.000
Now, Slack is fighting for its life.
How did Slack lose, while in most ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:43.760
it was better than Microsoft Teams?
And… will Slack survive?

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.880
One of the strangest parts of this story&nbsp;
is that Slack was sort of an accident.&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.560
It was an internal tool at&nbsp;
a game studio: Tiny Speck.&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.200
When their game failed in 2012, co-founder&nbsp;
Stewart Butterfield had an idea.&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.920
The messaging tool was really good.
It was clear to Butterfield that Slack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:09.120
as it was now called, was solving a problem,&nbsp;
and oh boy, was this massive problem.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:15.200
I want you to imagine a world 15 years ago.
There's no Teams, Slack, Discord, or any group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.680
messaging apps. All businesses were just using&nbsp;
email. And the only alternative was meetings.&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.960
For one-on-one conversations, yeah, not&nbsp;
bad, but as soon as you need two, three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.240
or more people, it was a nightmare.
Forwarding, CCing, BCCing people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.400
just scrolling up the conversation… Collaborating&nbsp;
was… and still is a nightmare inside email.&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.760
There was a huge opportunity, which brings us&nbsp;
back to Tiny Speck, now Slack Technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.280
who made their new platform public in August 2013.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.920
There was a certain vibe to Slack.
It felt like the new, cool, upstart tech product.&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.360
Startups, small and medium businesses,&nbsp;
loved Slack. Especially in Silicon Valley.&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.440
A slick UX, custom emojis, great and robust file&nbsp;
sharing, searchable chats, quick communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.160
threaded conversations, channels — it had it all.
Slack began exploding, but not in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.200
you might expect.
This software was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.560
really good. Everyone in tech was saying&nbsp;
the same thing: "You have to use Slack".&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.320
Slack didn't have an outbound&nbsp;
sales team until 2016.&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.560
It was growing by word of mouth.
"We begged and cajoled our friends at other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.840
companies to try it out and give us feedback."
On day one, they had 8,000 users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.040
then 15,000 two weeks later.
Slack was growing fast.&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.960
They reached a $1.1 billion valuation in 8 months.
It was the "fastest-growing business app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:05.600
ever" — at least, according to Slack.
And before they even had a CMO.&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.800
Yet, even when they did have sales staff,&nbsp;
their technique was, honestly, pretty great.&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.760
They pitched Slack not to a top executive like&nbsp;
a CTO or CIO, but to a regular team manager.&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.040
"For small organizations, team and company&nbsp;
may be one and the same. But if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.440
an organization of 15,000 people, you end up&nbsp;
with a situation like Adobe, with nine paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.920
Slack teams. Mid-level managers could say, 'This&nbsp;
thing sounds cool, let's try it out for our team.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.240
If they liked it, it was affordable enough to&nbsp;
just expense it." (Stewart Butterfield, 2015).&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.160
That's great. Building for the end&nbsp;
user first. Yet, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:51.040
this would come back to bite Slack down the line.
Nonetheless, for now, Slack was unstoppable.&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.640
In February 2014, it had 15,000 daily&nbsp;
users. The same time the following year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.840
that was over 500,000, with 135,000 paid users.
They crossed 2 million by the end of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.680
then 4 million in October&nbsp;
2016, less than a year later.&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:14.240
By January 2019, Slack had passed 10 million&nbsp;
daily users, with $400 million in revenue.&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.720
The same goes for funding too.
$42 million in 2014, then $110&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.240
million later that year.
$172 million in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:29.200
$208 million in 2016, $412 million in&nbsp;
2017, then another $427 million in 2018!&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.360
They were gearing up for an IPO too.
Everything was going great…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.480
at least, on the outside.
Behind the scenes, a huge battle was taking place.&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:48.240
And as many stories go, it got&nbsp;
worse when Microsoft stepped in.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.800
One of the biggest problems facing those&nbsp;
in software is actually something called&nbsp;&nbsp;

