00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.960
Let's go back to 2016. Slack was&nbsp;
exploding, and Microsoft wanted in.&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.760
In March 2016, Microsoft considered&nbsp;
buying Slack for $8 billion.&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.320
But they didn’t. Why?
Bill Gates.&nbsp;

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.880
He believed Microsoft didn't need Slack.&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.480
Rather, he argued Microsoft should just&nbsp;
build a competitor… on top of… Skype.&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:38.160
A twist, I know. Skype? Really?
But here's some important context.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:44.480
Microsoft had just spent $8.5 billion&nbsp;
on Skype, and it wasn't going too well.&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.280
This app wasn't really making any money.
They tried to make the platform a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.840
platform, as well as a personal one.
It wasn't working.&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:59.600
But they had the template right in front of them.
Why not make Skype the new Slack?&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:04.800
Skype Teams, as it was called, already&nbsp;
had different channels, aka chat rooms.&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:09.440
It was just lacking a few special bits&nbsp;
from Slack, like threaded conversations.&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:14.640
It would soon join the Office 365 suite as part&nbsp;
of the monthly subscription for businesses.&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.560
But things didn't go as planned.
Skype was… well… not very good.&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:26.400
It was an old app already, its brand was messy,&nbsp;
and users were having all kinds of problems.&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.160
So Microsoft went back to the drawing&nbsp;
board. Cut Skype, build from the ground up:&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.160
Teams.
Skype Teams, just without Skype.&nbsp;

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:41.120
And this app was a huge blow to Slack.
Because Teams was actually a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.000
well-built product.
Slack was better in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.760
lot of ways. It had way more app integrations,
had a better developer experience, a nicer UX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.720
but Teams was better in ways that mattered more.
It was almost as good as Slack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.880
and it also did something better.
Small to medium companies loved it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:05.760
especially in Silicon Valley, but the big players&nbsp;
— aka the ones with deep pockets — not so much.&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.400
Giants like Uber, even after using&nbsp;
Slack, eventually ditched it.&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.240
Teams was in the perfect spot.
It had voice, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.640
video — aka video conferencing — plus&nbsp;
dial-in calling, and PSTN integration.&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.400
This was definitely a weak spot of Slack's. Many&nbsp;
companies using Slack would also use Google Meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.760
or other software.
But there was more.&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:33.280
Uber ditched Slack, not due to features,&nbsp;
but for another reason: security.&nbsp;

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:39.280
Slack had done an SOC 2 audit, but didn't&nbsp;
have compliance for PCI-DSS or HIPAA.&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.950
Microsoft did, and more. (Show on screen in a list&nbsp;
side by side: Microsoft: SOC I and 2, ISO 27001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.960
HIPAA, EU Model Clauses. Slack: SOC 2, ISO 27001).
Teams was more secure, and for big corporations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:52.160
this was very important. For some, like a&nbsp;
hospital or bank, this is just a hard requirement.&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.480
But Microsoft wasn't just coming for&nbsp;
enterprise. It was coming for everything.&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.000
Slack was a better sell to end&nbsp;
users and middle management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.160
but Teams was a better sell to&nbsp;
heads of IT and heads of companies.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:09.600
Teams didn't need to be a better app. Because&nbsp;
Microsoft didn't build it for the end user.&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.840
The biggest difference, by far, was that&nbsp;
Teams was included in 365. That's it.&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.160
Teams was force-installed for&nbsp;
every Office 365 subscriber.&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.680
Millions of enterprise users, overnight.
And very quickly, managers everywhere did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:32.080
the math: We already have Teams in&nbsp;
365… why are we paying for Slack?&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:48.320
But this isn't the end of the story.
Slack wasn't about to roll over on this.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.800
Like Slack, we’re still trying to&nbsp;
grow. So if you could subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.080
it’d mean a lot! We’re almost at 1&nbsp;
million, and we need your help. Thanks.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:06.080
On November 2nd, 2016, readers saw an&nbsp;
entire, full-page ad titled "Dear Microsoft."

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:10.400
It was a long, extensive list of&nbsp;
"advice" to Microsoft on how to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:17.840
good product — aka, why Slack was so good.
"One final point: Slack is here to stay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:23.280
We are where work happens for millions of people&nbsp;
around the world. We're glad you're going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.240
helping us define this new product category. We&nbsp;
admire many of your achievements and know you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.960
be a worthy competitor."
Slack also published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.200
the same letter on their website.
Slack was ready for a fight. But they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.000
were certainly a bit rattled.
And for good reason.&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.120
Teams added 31 million daily users in&nbsp;
a single month in 2020. For context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.120
Slack had 12 million total.
There was a great exodus.&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.560
Thousands of companies were leaving the colorful&nbsp;
UI for that bleak, corporate, purple knock-off.&nbsp;

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.000
Unfortunately, their opponent&nbsp;
had broken the rules.&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.200
Instead of competing for customers by having the&nbsp;
best product — aka, the free market — Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.360
just bundled Teams with something hundreds&nbsp;
of millions were already paying for.&nbsp;

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:10.560
Microsoft didn't "win people over" from&nbsp;
Slack. They were trying to kill Slack.&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:16.800
So, Slack went to war.
[Slack Complaint, 2020]:&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.760
"Microsoft is reverting to past behavior. They&nbsp;
created a weak, copycat product and tied it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:49.680
their dominant Office product, force installing it&nbsp;
and blocking its removal — a carbon copy of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:54.880
illegal behavior during the 'browser wars.'"
"Slack simply wants fair competition and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:59.200
level playing field. Healthy competition&nbsp;
drives innovation and creates the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:04.960
products and the most choice for customers.&nbsp;
We're asking the EU to be a neutral referee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:10.960
examine the facts, and enforce the law."
Butterfield also said "Microsoft is perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:16.400
unhealthily preoccupied with killing us,&nbsp;
and Teams is the vehicle to do that."&nbsp;

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:21.920
But the EU, being a government body,&nbsp;
was very slow. It would be years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.320
before they got to Slack's complaint.
And Slack was running out of time.&nbsp;

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.600
Every day, more businesses left Slack&nbsp;
for Teams, and Slack's revenue fell.&nbsp;

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.880
Then came an unexpected rescue.
Butterfield had met with Bret Taylor, Co-CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:42.480
of Salesforce, about potentially buying Quip&nbsp;
from them. Though the discussion soon flipped.&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:51.200
In late 2020, Salesforce would buy Slack for over&nbsp;
$27 billion — their largest acquisition ever.&nbsp;

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:57.520
Slack would be integrated into Customer&nbsp;
360 — aka Salesforce's own 365 suite.&nbsp;

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:02.240
This could save Slack, but&nbsp;
then came an unexpected hurdle.&nbsp;

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:09.280
Exactly two years later, Bret Taylor resigned.
Just days later, Butterfield also resigned.&nbsp;

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:13.760
Slack's own chief product officer&nbsp;
and SVP of marketing followed.&nbsp;

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:16.960
Why?
First, Butterfield.&nbsp;

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:19.760
He had a number of obligations&nbsp;
after the acquisition.&nbsp;

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.200
And it seems like those&nbsp;
lasted for exactly two years.&nbsp;

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:27.760
So, when his contractual obligations from&nbsp;
the acquisition were up, he stepped away.&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:31.440
And it's hard to blame him:
"Butterfield's exit also has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:36.720
do with Salesforce asserting itself more with&nbsp;
Slack, and as that happens, other longtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.040
executives are taking the opportunity to leave."
[Jason Wong, Analyst]: "It's clear that Salesforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.400
is taking Slack in a direction&nbsp;
that is more aligned to extending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:50.400
and enabling their Customer 360 Platform."
Bret Taylor, though, was a bit more worrying.&nbsp;

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:54.880
He was the architect of the acquisition.&nbsp;
His vision was for Salesforce to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.880 --> 00:15:00.240
a "digital HQ", with Slack at the center.
But Taylor leaving at the same time signaled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:06.160
that the bet might not be working.
Microsoft 365 was just too strong.&nbsp;

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:10.240
Salesforce stock fell 8.3% the&nbsp;
day after Taylor's resignation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:17.360
then another 7.4% the day Butterfield resigned.
The whole year, it had lost 47% of its value.&nbsp;

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:24.080
And, ironically, replacing Taylor was Lidiane&nbsp;
Jones, who had spent twelve years at Microsoft.&nbsp;

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:28.560
Speaking of Microsoft, there was&nbsp;
some good news for Slack at last.&nbsp;

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:33.040
In 2023, the EU had finally gotten&nbsp;
around to their investigation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.480
and it wasn't in Microsoft's favor.
But something unexpected happened.&nbsp;

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:44.720
Microsoft knew they were in the wrong. So they&nbsp;
quietly separated Teams from 365 in Europe.&nbsp;

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:48.880
Then globally in 2024.
Then in 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.840
the European Commission made their verdict.
Teams had to stay unbundled for 7 years.&nbsp;

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:57.600
But it could be offered at&nbsp;
a discount to 365 customers.&nbsp;

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:01.760
The penalties were up to 10% of&nbsp;
Microsoft's worldwide revenue.&nbsp;

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:05.600
But, there was no fine.
Microsoft just had to separate it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.240
years after the damage was done.
[Marc Benioff, Salesforce CEO]: "You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.560
can see the horrible things that Microsoft&nbsp;
did to Slack before we bought it. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:17.120
pretty bad and they were running their&nbsp;
playbook and did a lot of dark stuff."

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:23.280
Despite all this, Slack isn't dying.&nbsp;
But its situation still isn't great.&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:31.040
Revenue in 2023 was $1.7 billion, up 17%.
They currently have 47 million daily active users.&nbsp;

00:16:31.040 --> 00:16:35.520
Slack still has a strong hold on niche&nbsp;
markets, like small businesses and startups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:41.680
and even some giants in the Fortune 100.
But the clear winner is Microsoft Teams.&nbsp;

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:46.080
They have 37% market share,&nbsp;
while Slack has just 13%.&nbsp;

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:50.240
Though, I don’t know many&nbsp;
people who actually love Teams.&nbsp;

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:54.960
It makes me a bit sad, what happened.
There are a lot of what ifs but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.960 --> 00:17:01.280
the end, Microsoft won by changing the rules.
Then quietly abiding by them when they got caught.&nbsp;

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:07.440
While Teams is killing Slack, Microsoft is also&nbsp;
killing one of their own beloved apps in 365.&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:13.040
Microsoft, weirdly, has two different mailing&nbsp;
apps. Now it's trying to combine them into one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

